// SOURCE for lib/std/core.fi -- GENERATED by tools/strlib/expand.py.
//
// ========================================================================
// std.core -- THE HALF OF THE LIBRARY THAT NEEDS NOTHING (round 73)
// ========================================================================
//
// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS. Since round 52 `import std.*` has been forbidden
// wholesale under `profile kernel` (SPEC 2). The ban was right and too
// coarse at the same time: a large part of the library asks nobody for
// memory and makes no system call. `Span.trim()`, `find`, `starts_with`,
// `compare`, the UTF-8 reader, `text_to_i64`, `digit_count` and the whole
// of `math` are arithmetic on memory that the CALLER holds. They fell
// under the ban only because they happened to stand in the same file as
// the functions that call `mmap`.
//
// WHY IT IS CALLED `core`. Rust cut the same line and calls the pieces
// `core` (needs nothing), `alloc` (needs an allocator) and `std` (needs an
// operating system). The name is the one every reader already knows, and
// it says what the module is: the core that is left when you take
// everything away that presumes a machine underneath. The alternatives
// were `std.base` (says nothing about the property), `std.pure` (promises
// freedom from side effects, which is not what this is about -- these
// functions write into foreign memory quite happily) and `std.free`
// (ambiguous with `free`). One collision is worth naming: the demo file
// `demos/freestanding/core.fi` is a module named `core` too. Both are root files
// of their own compilation and never meet; whoever writes a kernel that
// wants BOTH renames his own.
//
// THE LINE ALONG WHICH IT IS CUT, in one sentence: what is in here makes
// no `syscall` and asks for no memory. In Firn those two are the same
// question -- every allocation in this library goes through `mmap`, that
// is, through `syscall` (lib/str/alloc.fi, lib/rt/rt.fi). The compiler
// checks the property; it does not believe this comment (compiler/src/
// prof.rs, round 73): the declaration `profile kernel` in the first line
// is the CLAIM, and because the module lands in the same compilation unit
// as the kernel that imports it, every `syscall` in it is an error at the
// place where it stands.
//
// WHAT IS IN HERE
//   1. memory work without an operating system  (lib/str/core_mem.fi)
//   2. the reading text facade: Span, searching, comparing, trimming,
//      splitting, UTF-8                          (lib/str/core_facade.fi,
//                                                 lib/str/core_impl.fi)
//   3. text -> number, and the bit patterns of f64
//                                                (lib/num/bignum.fi,
//                                                 lib/num/strtod.fi,
//                                                 lib/num/core_bits.fi,
//                                                 lib/num/core_facade.fi,
//                                                 lib/num/core_comfort.fi)
//   4. mathematics, whole                        (lib/math/core_math.fi)
//   5. the Allocator, passed by hand             (lib/mem/core_alloc.fi)
//
// WHAT IS NOT IN HERE, and why
//   * `Bytes`, `Str16`, `AtomTable`, `Vec`, `Map` -- they OWN memory.
//   * everything with `rt.heap_alloc` -- that is mmap(2).
//   * everything with `io.*` -- that is SYS_WRITE / SYS_READ.
//   * `dtoa` (number -> text) -- it writes into a `Bytes`.
//
// THE OLD NAMES KEEP WORKING. `std.str`, `std.num` and `std.math` are
// unchanged from the outside: they include the very same files. There is
// ONE source text per function, and the include library (lib/str, lib/num,
// lib/math) stays what it has always been.
//
// TWO `Span` TYPES, said out loud: whoever imports `std.core` AND
// `std.str` into one program gets `core.Span` and `str.Span` as two
// distinct types with the same shape. That is the price of a stage 0
// without type aliases (SPEC 14). It costs nothing in practice, because
// the two modules are meant for two different profiles -- a kernel has no
// `std.str`, and an application has no reason for `std.core`.

// The module holds to the rules of the kernel profile, and it says so
// here. That is not decoration: `compiler/src/prof.rs` (and its twin in
// `bin/firnc1.fi`) reads this line and admits `import std.core` under
// `profile kernel` because of it -- and the whole apparatus of the kernel
// profile then checks the claim on this file, because it lands in the same
// compilation unit as the kernel. A `syscall` smuggled in here is an error
// at the line where it stands (tests/neg/core_kernel_syscall.fi).
profile kernel

